# Normalise line endings in the repository, not in the working tree.
#
# Without this, git applies the platform default on checkout, so the same commit
# produces different bytes on Windows and Linux. The practical cost is churn that
# looks like a real change: `git status` reports modified files that were never
# edited, and every diff mixes line-ending noise into substantive edits.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one file that must stay CRLF, because Windows tooling expects it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Snapshots, media and generated bundles are binary. Saying so stops git from
# trying to normalise them and corrupting the bytes — and stops diffs of a
# refreshed baseline from showing as "text file changed".
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary

# Screenshots are regenerated per platform, and CI compares the Linux ones.
tests/**/*-linux.png binary
tests/**/*-win32.png binary

# Generated artifacts. Kept tracked on purpose — they are the evidence this site
# exists to publish, and a reviewer must be able to diff them without a build.
registry/*.json text eol=lf
public/og/*.png binary

# Lockfiles and anything reviewed as a diff rather than edited.
bun.lock text eol=lf